{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And Israel saw the great hand.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'hand' means a blow, in the manner of (Exodus 9:3): ״הנה יד ה' הויה״, (I Samuel 5:11): ״כבדה מאד יד האלהים״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash —</b> 'the great hand': from here the Rabbis of blessed memory derived that at the sea they were struck with fifty plagues. The reason is that in the ten plagues we find written ״אצבע אלהים היא״, and since ten plagues are one 'finger,' and the hand is five fingers, and upon each and every finger there are ten plagues — behold, they are fifty plagues." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah —</b> 'the great hand' is the final ה of the Name, and it is the attribute of mild judgment below, called Kingdom, by means of which the sea was split. And therefore we say in prayer: ״מלכותך ראו בניך בוקע ים לפני משה זה אלי ענו הודו והמליכו״ — a hint to this is ״אלי ואנוהו״. And you already know that when they passed through the sea they said the Song. And the reason it is called 'great' is that it draws from חסד, for thus it is mentioned in the Song in the expression ״ימינך״." "<b>And you should reflect</b> on the translation of Onkelos: ״ית גבורת ידא רבתא״ — for he translates 'the great hand,' only that he added to the verse the expression 'might' (גבורה), to hint that the might within it, which is the attribute of judgment, struck the Egyptians, while the חסד within it saved Israel. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote in his commentary, and this is his language: 'And by way of the truth one should say that the great hand was revealed to them, and it is the attribute of judgment that YHWH performed in Egypt, for there was a plague upon them in Egypt, and this is like (Deuteronomy 4:36): ״ועל הארץ הראך את אשו הגדולה״, and it is the right hand with which You crush the enemy, and it is the arm of YHWH of which Scripture says (Isaiah 51:9): ״עורי עורי לבשי עוז זרוע ה' הלא את היא המחרבת ים״, and what is said לבשי עוז will not be hidden from you in light of what we wrote on the verse (Genesis 49:24): ״מידי אביר יעקב״.' End of quote." "<b>And the people feared and believed.</b> Fear and faith are the foundation of the entire Torah. And Rabbeinu Chananel wrote that faith is divided into four parts: faith in the Holy One, blessed be He, as it says (II Chronicles 20:20): ״האמינו בה' אלהיכם ותאמנו״, and this is what is written: ״ויאמינו בה' ובמשה עבדו״; faith in the prophets, for we are obligated to heed their words, as it says (Isaiah 53:1): ״מי האמין לשמועתנו״; faith in the World to Come, for one must believe that there is a World to Come and there is good reward prepared for the righteous, and this is what David, of blessed memory, said (Psalms 27:13): ״לולא האמנתי לראות בטוב ה'״; faith in the coming of the redeemer, which is a great principle in the Torah, as it says (Isaiah 28:16): ״הנני יסד בציון אבן אבן בחן פנת יקרת מוסד מוסד המאמין לא יחיש״. And whoever believes has many merits; thus it is said of Abraham, who was the root of faith (Genesis 15:6): ״והאמין בה' ויחשבה לו צדקה״ — and behold, he merits the Garden of Eden, as it says (Isaiah 26:2): ״פתחו שערים ויבא גוי צדיק שומר אמונים״, and he merits the life of the World to Come, as it says (Habakkuk 2:4): ״וצדיק באמונתו יחיה״. And whoever does not believe in all these, concerning him Scripture says (Job 15:22): ״לא יאמין שוב מני חשך וצפוי הוא אלי חרב״, and he does not merit redemption, as it says (Ezekiel 20:37–38): ״וברותי מכם המורדים והפושעים בי״, and it is written (Psalms 31:24): ״אמונים נוצר ה' ומשלם על יתר עשי גאוה״ — meaning that HaShem, blessed be He, guards the faithful, and upon those who have no faith He pays back their punishment, for they act in arrogance, and they do not merit life, as it says (Habakkuk 2:4): ״הנה עופלה לא ישרה נפשו בו וצדיק באמונתו יחיה״.' End of the Rav's words, of blessed memory." "<b>And because</b> faith is the foundation of the entire Torah, our Rabbis of blessed memory enacted for us in prayer and in blessings to answer Amen, which is derived from the language of faith (אמונה) and from the language of acknowledgement (הודאה), that one accepts upon himself the words of the one who blesses and acknowledges them. And this is what they said, may their memory be blessed: Amen — acceptance; Amen — oath; Amen — fulfillment. And concerning this they said: Greater is the one who answers Amen than the one who blesses." "<b>And the explanation of the matter</b> is that the one who blesses testifies in his blessing of the Holy One, blessed be He, that He is the source of blessing, and the one who answers Amen upholds the deed, and he is the essential thing — for the testimony of the first witness has no validity except through a second witness, by whom the testimony is completed. And the one who answers Amen is the second witness, and he must join with the first witness, who is the one who blesses, for with him the testimony is established. And our Rabbis of blessed memory already spoke at length concerning the rule of pronouncing the letters of this word and how a person is obligated to enunciate them, as they said: one may not answer neither a cut-off Amen nor a snatched Amen nor an orphaned Amen, but a prolonged Amen. Ben Azzai says: the one who answers a snatched Amen — his days shall be snatched; a cut-off Amen — his days shall be cut off; an orphaned Amen — his children shall be orphans; a prolonged Amen — his days and years are prolonged for him. And the meaning of חטופה is that he snatches the אל\"ף with a שוא and a פתח, and behold this is the opposite of faith, as it is written concerning Amnon (II Samuel 13:20): ״האמינון אחיך היה עמך״, and Rashi of blessed memory explained: דשקרי\"ר in the vernacular (i.e., a lie). From this you learn that the one who answers a snatched Amen — behold, he turns the bowl upside down and brings down to the earth the foundations of the walls of faith. Behold, I have explained the meaning of Amen because it is derived from the language of faith (אמונה), besides which there is in the word Amen a mystery that includes the unique Name and the appellation and the Tiferet and the Kavod." "<b>And there is</b> in it yet another received mystery: the word Amen is the joining of ten Sefirot — from the אל\"ף, which is Keter, which is the flowing source, to the מ\"ם, which is Teshuvah, and which is the King of the World; and from the מ\"ם to the נו\"ן, which is the Shekhinah, which is called in feminine language and embraces male and female — and the allusion is in the bent נו\"ן and the straight נו\"ן. And since the drawing down of blessings reaches the נו\"ן, from there the blessing spreads to the world of separate intelligences, and from them to this lowly world. And just as it begins from the source alluded to in the letter אל\"ף and draws the blessing from there, like one who opens a spring to water those who need watering, so too the one who answers Amen receives his reward — that the gates of the Garden of Eden are opened for him, as it is said (Isaiah 26:2): ״פתחו שערים ויבא גוי צדיק שומר אמונים״ — do not read אמונים but rather אמנים, these are the ones who answer Amen; and behold his reward is with him, measure for measure. And you already know that the word Amen is a notarikon: אל מלך נאמן — El, King, faithful." "<b>And you should be roused to ask:</b> in the word נאמן, why is there a bent נו\"ן and a straight נו\"ן — not to obscure but to explain. And the straight נו\"ן in the word אמן embraces male and female, because the bent נו\"ן is female and the straight נו\"ן is male; and since the bent נו\"ן is included within the straight one, therefore it embraces male and female." "<b>And you should know</b> that the אל\"ף is called Amen, and Wisdom (חכמה) is called Amon, as it is said (Proverbs 8:30): ״ואהיה אצלו אמון״, and it is all one, for one may not separate Wisdom from Keter. And since the blessing comes from there, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 134:2–3): ״שאו ידיכם קדש״ — this is Wisdom — ״וברכו את ה׳״; therefore greater is the one who answers Amen than the one who blesses. And this is in the border-towns, but in the Sanctuary they would not answer Amen, for they had no need, because they would pronounce the Name as written. And the two first letters allude to the source of the blessing, and therefore there is no need to mention Amen in the Sanctuary. And whoever intends and doubles Amen Amen, merits two worlds. And so said David, peace be upon him, at the end of Psalm 72: ״ברוך ה׳ אלהים אלהי ישראל עושה נפלאות לבדו וברוך שם כבודו לעולם וימלא כבודו את כל הארץ אמן ואמן״, and furthermore in Psalm 89: ״ברוך ה׳ לעולם אמן ואמן״. And this is the one who honors HaShem, blessed be He, in truth, concerning whom Scripture said (I Samuel 2:30): ״כי מכבדי אכבד״, and anyone who is not careful with the word Amen to intend with all his power and intention — behold, he disgraces the Shaddai, and concerning him Scripture said (ibid.): ״ובוזי יקלו״. And from the seven compartments that are in Gehinnom, they lower him to the last of them all, which is doubled, and it is called (Job 10:22): ״ארץ עיפתה״ — and it is the nethermost earth of Sheol, for Sheol is one compartment and תחתית is a second compartment, and that תחתית is called Abaddon, as it is said (Proverbs 15:11): ״שאול ואבדון נגד ה׳״. One who descends to Abaddon never again ascends. And concerning this Scripture said (Jeremiah 2:13): ״אותי עזבו מקור מים חיים״ — those who disdain answering Amen have forsaken the source of living waters, which is the flowing spring alluded to above; and what will be their punishment? ״לחצוב להם בארות בארות נשברים״ — that is to say, they descend to Gehinnom level after level in a doubled punishment, and this is why it doubled ״בארות בארות״; and it is all measure for measure, both in punishment and in reward."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:51.555508366Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}